WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.410
- It's almost time to go, y'all.

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.510
But before we do, I have
one more story to share.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:04.552
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:04.552 --> 00:00:07.140
(upbeat music)
(audience clapping)

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.900
Last month, an author in
Ohio took to Twitter to vent

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.810
about a recent book signing
with a disappointing turnout.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.700
She wrote, "Only two people came

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.170
to my author signing yesterday,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.910
so I was pretty bummed about it.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:22.170
Especially as 37 people
responded, 'going', to the event.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.780
Kind of upset, honestly,
and a little embarrassed."

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.370
That author was Chelsea Banning,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.107
and she was promoting her
self-published debut novel,

00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:30.780
"Of Crowns And Legends".

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.040
I think any artist can relate

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.440
to that kind of disappointment at a show.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.420
But what happened next is truly amazing.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.253
It's very cool.

00:00:37.253 --> 00:00:40.260
The post went viral, and
authors like Stephen King,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.930
Margaret Atwood, and Neil Gaiman chimed in

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.480
with their book signing
horror stories as well.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.910
The best part, "Of Crowns And Legends"

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.180
rocketed up the Amazon
charts, not too shabby.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.830
We have Chelsea joining us now.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:53.695
What's up Chelsea?

00:00:53.695 --> 00:00:55.800
(audience clapping)
(audience cheering)

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.633
- Hi, Kelly!

00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:57.600
How are you?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.560
- I am so good!

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.830
I love how this story turned out,

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.600
'cause how bad was this signing?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.910
- Oh, it was pretty disheartening

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.770
because I was there a few hours,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.380
and only a couple people showed up,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.890
and I was still feeling
sad on the way home

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.790
and the next morning, and I
just really wanted to talk

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.890
to other writers about it.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.280
So, I went to Twitter to kind
of just vent into the void,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.890
not really thinking anyone's gonna see it.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.280
And then a couple hours
later, it went viral,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.420
and there's all these
amazing authors chiming in

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.850
with their own signing horror stories.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.940
Like you said, Neil Gaiman,
Stephen King, Margaret Atwood,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.000
and so many more, and
even other celebrities.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.340
And it was just so heartwarming

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.850
and encouraging, and truly a blessing.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.110
- Well, it is important to note

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.120
that everyone thinks it happens overnight.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.000
Even with me, I did things
before "American Idol",

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.167
and there are disappointing
turnouts, and you think,

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:57.870
"Oh my God, should I keep doing this?"

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.310
And it can be very discouraging.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.560
So, it's important to
tell people these stories,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.390
and it's also important for people

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.130
like the Stephen King's,
and Margaret Atwood's,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.290
to join in and say, "This
happened to me as well."

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.790
It's a very cool thing,

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.280
and congratulations on writing
a book in the first place

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.567
and sharing your voice with the world.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:14.850
(audience clapping)
That's an incredible thing

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:15.750
to write a novel.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.880
So, tell us what it's about.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.120
- So, it is a King Arthur re-imagining.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.840
It takes place 20 years
after King Arthur's death,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.780
and it follows the twin
children he and Guinevere

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.980
were never supposed to have.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.840
So, they weren't supposed to exist.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.620
And it follows their
adventures, there's magic.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.780
You'll meet new characters,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.540
you'll see old characters
from Arthurian Myth,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.610
like Guinevere, Merlin, and all of them.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.040
It's book one of three.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:44.873
- Oh, it's a trilogy.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:45.706
That's cool.

00:02:45.706 --> 00:02:48.360
- As you said, it soared
to the Amazon charts

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.560
in Arthurian Fantasy,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.570
and now I even have another signing

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.198
at the same bookstore later this month.

00:02:53.198 --> 00:02:55.860
(audience clapping)
- Ah, that's awesome.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.180
I love these kind of stories.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.670
So, I can't wait to read
it, I'm so proud of you.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.301
I'm liking your post right now, alright.

00:03:01.301 --> 00:03:02.730
(bell rings)

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.430
- [Chelsea] Oh, thank you so much!

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.810
- And that's not all, girl.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.330
Our season long partner,
Pilot Pen, makers of G2,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.890
the go-to pen of "The
Kelly Clarkson Show",

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.560
want to honor fearless
creators, just like you.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.610
So they're gonna be writing you a check

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.540
for a thousand dollars.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.466
How's that sound?

00:03:16.466 --> 00:03:17.984
(audience cheering)
- What?

00:03:17.984 --> 00:03:18.817
No way!

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.900
- [Kelly] I cannot wait
to read your books.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.103
Congratulations, girl!
(audience clapping)

